Elasticsearch is a distributed search and analytics database optimized for indexed documents.

It is not a traditional relational database.
Instead of rows and tables, it stores JSON documents.

Instead of storing logs as plain text, it indexes them.

What is an Index?
In MySQL:

Database
↓
Table
↓
Rows

In ElasticSearch:

Cluster
↓
Index
↓
Documents


Example log:
  INFO Login Successful

Stored as a document:
  {
  "timestamp":"2026-07-31",
  "level":"INFO",
  "message":"Login Successful",
  "namespace":"default",
  "pod":"java-app"
 }

 Every log becomes a searchable document.



 Why Elasticsearch?

Suppose you have:
  500 million logs

Searching plain text files could be slow.

Elasticsearch builds indexes so queries like:
  ERROR or UserID=1001 or Last 10 minutes
can return results quickly.


